# WITH CLAUDE — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; never commit real values.
#
# ── THE ONE RULE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing here is ever prefixed `PUBLIC_`. Astro inlines any `PUBLIC_*`
# variable into the browser bundle, so a `PUBLIC_DATABASE_URL` would hand the
# production connection string to every visitor. `db/env.ts` refuses to start
# if one exists, and `tests/security.test.ts` checks the built client bundle
# for connection strings. Every variable below is server-side only.


# ── DATABASE (required) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# PostgreSQL on Neon. Used by `/api/submit` at request time, and by the
# migration and import commands from a terminal.
#
# Neon → your project → Connection Details → connection string (pooled).
# Keep `?sslmode=require`.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@ep-example-123456.ap-southeast-1.aws.neon.tech/withclaude?sslmode=require"


# ── SUBMISSION PRIVACY (required) ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Mixed into a submitter's IP address before it is hashed, so the stored value
# is useful for rate limiting and useless for identifying anybody. Without a
# salt, a hashed IPv4 address is reversible in seconds — the whole space is
# four billion entries.
#
# Generate one and never rotate it casually: changing it resets every existing
# rate-limit counter.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
SUBMISSION_IP_SALT="replace-me-with-64-random-hex-characters"


# ── ACKNOWLEDGEMENT EMAIL (optional in development) ───────────────────────
#
# Resend sends the one transactional email this site has: "we have your
# submission, a person will read it, nothing is published yet".
#
# If these are unset, the endpoint still validates, still stores the
# submission, and returns 202 — but it does NOT pretend to have sent anything.
# It logs that the email was skipped and the response says
# `acknowledgementSent: false`, which the form panel surfaces rather than
# promising an email that is never going to arrive.
#
# In production, unset credentials are logged as an error on every submission.
#
# RESEND_FROM must be an address on a domain verified in Resend.
RESEND_API_KEY=""
RESEND_FROM="WITH CLAUDE <hello@withclaude.in>"
# Optional. Where replies to the acknowledgement go, if not RESEND_FROM.
RESEND_REPLY_TO=""


# ── NIGHTLY REBUILD (production only) ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# Event lifecycle is derived from the clock, so the static site has to rebuild
# even when no code changes. `vercel.json` calls `/api/cron/rebuild` at
# 22:30 UTC — 04:00 IST — and that route POSTs to a deploy hook.
#
# CRON_SECRET is set automatically by Vercel when a cron is configured; the
# route refuses to run without it, so the URL is not a free build button.
# Create the deploy hook at: Project Settings → Git → Deploy Hooks.
CRON_SECRET=""
VERCEL_DEPLOY_HOOK_URL=""


# ── WHERE THE PUBLIC SITE READS ITS RECORDS FROM (Phase 3) ────────────────
#
# The public build has two possible sources for the same data:
#
#   ts   the TypeScript record in `src/data/*.ts`   ← the default
#   db   PostgreSQL, read once before the build
#
# THE DEFAULT IS `ts`, AND THAT IS THE ROLLBACK. If a database-backed build
# ever turns out to be wrong in production, changing this one variable back to
# `ts` restores the previous behaviour with no code change and no deploy of
# anything but a rebuild. Leave it unset and you get `ts`.
#
# An unrecognised value is a hard error rather than a silent fallback, so a
# typo cannot leave you believing you tested the database path.
#
# The database is read ONCE, by `npm run prebuild`, which writes
# `.astro/dataset.json`. Nothing renders from a live connection: by the time
# `astro build` starts there is no database client in the process at all. That
# is what keeps the site static and keeps PostgreSQL away from the browser.
DATA_SOURCE="ts"

# A read-only role for the public build. STRONGLY PREFERRED when DATA_SOURCE=db.
#
# The build only ever SELECTs, so the credential it runs with should only be
# able to SELECT — a build cannot corrupt the record it is reading if the role
# it connects as has no INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE or DDL to give. The admin keeps
# the write-capable DATABASE_URL; this is the public side's.
#
# Falls back to DATABASE_URL with a warning if unset, so a build never breaks
# for want of it. To create the role in Neon (or any PostgreSQL):
#
#   CREATE ROLE withclaude_build WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '…';
#   GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE neondb TO withclaude_build;
#   GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO withclaude_build;
#   GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO withclaude_build;
#   ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public
#     GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO withclaude_build;
#
# Note what is NOT granted: nothing on `submissions`, `city_interest`, `users`
# or `sessions` is needed by the public build, and revoking SELECT on those
# four is worth doing — the build has no use for a submitter's email address.
DATABASE_URL_READONLY=""

# Never prefix either connection string with PUBLIC_. Astro inlines anything
# PUBLIC_-prefixed into the browser bundle, and `db/env.ts` refuses to start if
# it finds one.


# ── TESTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The database tests run against PGlite — PostgreSQL compiled to WebAssembly,
# in-process — so they need no server, no credentials and no Docker. Nothing
# below is required to run `npm test`.
